# REST Endpoints

Every HTTP endpoint the theme exposes is a class in `public/class-api-{concern}.php`
extending the abstract `Api` base. There is no second way to do this: no
`register_rest_route()` at file scope in an `includes/` function file, no
`admin-ajax.php` handler, no route namespace other than `skaflow/v1`.

The procedural pattern — routes as `includes/**/rest-api/*.php` files pulled in by
`require_once` — **was removed on 2026-08-17.** If you find a plan or comment
describing it, it is stale. It fataled the site whenever a fork deleted the
capability that owned those files.

## The shape

```php
<?php

namespace Skaflow;

if ( ! defined( 'ABSPATH' ) ) {
	exit;
}

if ( class_exists( 'Skaflow\Api_Thing' ) ) {
	return;
}

class Api_Thing extends Api {

	protected function is_available(): bool {
		return class_exists( 'WooCommerce' );
	}

	public function register_routes(): void {
		$this->route(
			'/thing/(?P<id>\d+)',
			[
				'methods'             => \WP_REST_Server::READABLE,
				'callback'            => [ $this, 'get_thing' ],
				'permission_callback' => '__return_true',
				'args'                => [
					'id' => [
						'required'          => true,
						'type'              => 'integer',
						'sanitize_callback' => 'absint',
					],
				],
			]
		);
	}

	public function get_thing( \WP_REST_Request $request ): \WP_REST_Response {
		return $this->respond( [ 'ok' => true ] );
	}

}
```

`register_routes()` declares routes and nothing else. Inherited from `Api`:

| Member | Does |
|---|---|
| `Api::REST_NAMESPACE` | `skaflow/v1`, defined once |
| `route( $route, $args )` | `register_rest_route()` inside that namespace |
| `respond( $data, $status = 200 )` | `WP_REST_Response` |
| `error( $code, $message, $status = 400 )` | `WP_Error` with a `skaflow_`-prefixed code |
| `is_available()` | Availability gate, returns `true` unless overridden |

Add to the base only what more than one endpoint needs. One endpoint's helper is a
private method on that endpoint.

## Two gates, not interchangeable

| | Question | Where |
|---|---|---|
| `is_available()` | Does the code this endpoint needs exist? | Override in the subclass |
| `permission_callback` | Is this request allowed right now? | Per route |

`is_available()` runs at `functions.php` load — after plugins, before
`after_setup_theme`. `class_exists( 'WooCommerce' )` is safe there.
`post_type_exists()` and anything reading ACF options are **not**: neither is
registered yet, so the gate would silently return false and the route would never
appear.

Login state, capabilities, and admin-flipped options belong in the
`permission_callback` regardless — they are per-request answers, and a route that
is gated off entirely can't return a useful 401 or 403.
`public/class-api-wishlist.php` is the reference: WooCommerce presence in
`is_available()`, the ACF on/off switch and `is_user_logged_in()` in
`check_permission()`.

## Registration

`Init::define_api_hooks()` is a flat list plus one `class_exists()` test:

```php
$endpoints = [
	Api_Product_Search::class, // commerce — delete with WooCommerce.
	Api_Thing::class,
];

foreach ( $endpoints as $endpoint ) {
	if ( class_exists( $endpoint ) ) {
		new $endpoint();
	}
}
```

That test is the point, not defensive clutter: a fork deletes an endpoint file and
the site still boots, the same delete-a-unit property the capability
`bootstrap.php` requires in `includes/init.php` have. Never replace it with per-capability `if` statements in
`Init`, and never give `Init` a property for an endpoint instance.

Run `composer dump-autoload` after adding a file — the autoloader is a `classmap`
over `lib` and `public`.

## Callbacks a plugin addresses by name

Endpoint logic attached to a plugin's template hooks stays a plain function in that
capability's `includes/` directory, because `add_action()`/`remove_action()` match
it by string and a method reference cannot be removed the same way.
`public/class-api-quick-view.php` with
`includes/woocommerce/quick-view-functions.php` is the reference pair: the class
owns the route, the swap, and the output buffer; the functions are what
`woocommerce_single_product_summary` calls.

## Calling an endpoint from JS

Take the URL from the localized config — never rebuild it from
`window.location.origin`, which breaks on subdirectory installs and non-pretty
permalinks. `Front::get_localize_script_data()` publishes both the URLs and a
`wp_rest` nonce on `window.Skaflow_Public`.

```js
const response = await fetch( config.wishlistToggleUrl, {
	method: 'POST',
	credentials: 'same-origin',
	headers: {
		'Content-Type': 'application/json',
		'X-WP-Nonce': config.nonce,
	},
	body: JSON.stringify( { productId } ),
} );
```

`credentials` and `X-WP-Nonce` are both required for any endpoint whose
`permission_callback` checks login state — without them WordPress treats the
request as anonymous and `is_user_logged_in()` returns false. Handle `401` as
"signed out", not as a failure. Reference: `src/js/shared/wishlist.js`.

## Verification

```bash
cd <repo root>
ddev wp eval 'do_action( "rest_api_init" );
  foreach ( array_keys( rest_get_server()->get_routes() ) as $r ) {
    if ( false !== strpos( $r, "skaflow" ) ) { echo $r . "\n"; }
  }'
```

A missing route means `is_available()` returned false, the class name is absent
from `define_api_hooks()`, or the autoloader is stale. Check in that order.

## Anti-patterns

- `register_rest_route()` anywhere outside a `public/` endpoint class
- A route namespace other than `Api::REST_NAMESPACE`
- `admin-ajax.php` for new work
- Front-end rendering, asset enqueuing, or anything not an endpoint in `public/`
- `post_type_exists()` or `get_field()` inside `is_available()`
- Per-capability `if` blocks in `Init::define_api_hooks()`
- Duplicating response shaping instead of using `respond()` / `error()`
- Rebuilding a REST URL in JS instead of reading the localized value

